     "result_text_tail": "oftware slides now say the path depends on new contracts. At 40% growth, cash falls to about $2.6M and breakeven slips to 2030.\n\n**Things to know before you use it**\n- **Some values in tables and number cards show one decimal** (for example \"\u2212$2.7\"), because the renderer can't be told to show two there. The charts and titles show two.\n- **One QA warning is left on purpose.** It flags the $27.5M acquisition figure on the cash slide. I kept it there because it shows the plan's $9.5M is understated.\n- **Rider minimum-pay rules are not priced into the losses.** The briefing pack doesn't say what the rules would be. The deck instead shows that each extra $1 an hour adds about $0.42 per order.\n- **The 40% growth case is my own sensitivity assumption.** It is marked as such in the workbook.\n\n**Choices I made for you**\n- **Audience:** the board, deciding whether to approve or reject the launch.\n- **Look:** the navy theme.\n- **Storyline and placeholders:** I approved the storyline myself. No placeholders were needed: all 132 numbers come from the briefing pack or are calculated from it, with live formulas in the workbook. All sources are the pack itself, so there were no web sources to re-check.\n\nNext, I can switch the theme, cut the deck down for a shorter board slot, or add a backup slide on the rider-pay sensitivity.\n\nFiles are in `deliverable/`:\n- parcelbird.pptx (20 slides, 13 of them content)\n- parcelbird-sources.xlsx\n- parcelbird.json (the spec, rebuilt by `work/build_spec.py`)"
    }
